WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.380
Validation belongs close to API boundaries.

2
00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:14.360
Before data crosses into trusted internal state, the API should check shape, range, encoding, units, size, and required fields according to the contract.

3
00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:18.480
Parsing or deserializing data is not the same as trusting it.

4
00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:28.180
A structured object can still contain an invalid count, unsupported mode, unexpected tenant, oversized allocation request, or value in the wrong unit.

5
00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:31.100
Define allowed values precisely.

6
00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:41.200
Ranges, offsets, sizes, indexes, timeouts, enum values, names, paths, and identifiers should have clear constraints.

7
00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:48.340
Ambiguous units such as bytes versus elements or seconds versus milliseconds should be represented explicitly.

8
00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:52.540
Externally influenced counts deserve special care.

9
00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:58.720
Validate before allocation, loop bounds, indexing, copying, or sensitive use.

10
00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:04.340
A value can be syntactically valid and still exceed the application's resource policy.

11
00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:07.180
Keep raw input separate from trusted state.

12
00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:16.320
One useful pattern is to validate loose input into a narrow domain type, then allow internal APIs to accept only that validated type.

13
00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:25.560
Internal APIs need validation too when data crosses component, privilege, tenant, parser, plugin, or language boundaries.

14
00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:28.460
Internal does not automatically mean trustworthy.

15
00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:39.780
Good validation produces clear outcomes: accepted and normalized into a safe shape, rejected with safe diagnostics, or deferred only when the contract explicitly says another layer owns the check.

16
00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:42.620
Validation should also be ordered carefully.

17
00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:50.160
Check sizes, counts, and numeric conversions before using them for allocation, indexing, or loop control.

18
00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:52.440
Preserve length with data.

19
00:01:52.540 --> 00:02:01.740
Passing a pointer without a length, a buffer without ownership context, or a string without encoding expectations makes later checks harder to verify.

20
00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:06.660
Where possible, convert validated input into narrow internal types.

21
00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:12.680
A ValidatedTenantId, ByteCount, or ParsedConfig can express that the boundary check already happened.

22
00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:18.160
This keeps later code simpler because it no longer has to rediscover whether raw input was acceptable.
